A conformant repository continues to work after VibeConform is removed because nothing the repository runs calls `vibe` (rule R-01) [1][3]. Its functionality relies instead on standard third-party tools like `task`, `lefthook`, `go`, `uv`, `ruff`, and `pnpm` [3].

To remove VibeConform completely, you must delete the following:

*   `vibe.yaml` [1];
*   The audit job: either `.github/workflows/audit.yml` (for GitHub) or the audit job from `.gitlab-ci.yml` (for GitLab), along with its check from branch protection rules so pull requests do not hang waiting for a non-existent check [1];
*   The setup skill in either `.claude/skills/vibeconform-setup/` or `.agents/skills/vibeconform-setup/` [1];
*   Optionally, if you also want to remove `vibe-guard`, you must delete the PreToolUse hook running it and the `vibe-guard relock` command at the start of the Stop hook from `.claude/settings.json`, along with `.claude/hooks/policy.json` [1].

If using GitLab CI specifically, you must also delete `.vibe/`, `.gitlab-ci.vibe.yml`, and `Taskfile.vibe.yml`; their removal causes the existing includes to match nothing and do nothing harmlessly [5].

